    kind: work_item  # 196e
    title: "bash io_boundary producer-hint leaks command_launch into the persisted map, contradicting the WI-fakuv/WI-puvun on-demand reframe"  # 196e
    status: todo_hard  # 196e
    priority: 3  # 196e
    parent:  # 196e
    tags:  # 196e
    - io_primitives  # 196e
    - io_boundary  # 196e
    - dogfood  # 196e
    - schema_drift  # 196e
    isbefore: []  # 196e
    duplicate_of: []  # 196e
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 196e
    pr_ref:  # 196e
    description: "## Origin\n\nFiled 2026-07-15 from the INV-lifik zero-entropy investigation. INV-lifik flagged `edge.meta.io_boundary` as a zero-entropy field (`command_launch` on 478 edges, self-corpus v5). Root-caused to a reframe residual, not a schema-bloat defect, and split out here so INV-lifik can re-verify.\n\n## Symptom\n\n`edge.meta.io_boundary` in a **persisted** behavior map carries only `command_launch`, from bash edges (dst `bash:id`, `bash:stat`, `bash:find`, …). All 478 persisted io_boundary edges are bash; `bash.py` (~line 534) hardcodes `meta.io_boundary='command_launch'` at analysis time.\n\n## The inconsistency\n\nPer the WI-fakuv / WI-puvun **io-boundary reframe** (both `done`), io_boundary is officially a **consumer-time-derived, NOT persisted** field: `io_boundary.compute_boundary_map` applies the per-language `io_primitives` YAML catalogs (which DO map `open`->`fs_read`, `socket`->`net_send`, etc.) only in the `io-boundaries` / `verify-claims` / `slice --io-boundary` paths, stamping `meta.io_boundary` in memory. WI-puvun's text: \"substrate has 0 producer-stamped boundary records vs CLI's 7891 derived.\"\n\nBut `bash.py` still producer-stamps `command_launch` (bash has no `io_primitives/bash.yaml` catalog, so the analyzer stamps directly). That stamp both (a) feeds the on-demand computer as a hint AND (b) leaks into the persisted map — so the persisted field is a misleading `command_launch`-only zero-entropy field, while the rich value-space (`fs_read`/`fs_write`/`net_send`/`net_recv`) is realized only at consumer-time and never persisted. On the self-corpus there are 245 untagged fs/net-ish call edges (incl. `python:builtins:0-0:open:external_symbol`) that a survey consumer never sees classified.\n\n## Candidate dispositions (design fork — touches the reframe)\n\n1. **Strip persisted io_boundary at serialization** — align with \"not a persisted field\". Risk: the bash `command_launch` stamp is load-bearing for the on-demand `io-boundaries` classification (no bash.yaml), so stripping from the persisted map would need the stamp preserved as a separate producer-hint channel, or a `bash.yaml` catalog added so compute_boundary_map re-derives it.\n2. **Add `io_primitives/bash.yaml`** mapping shell commands -> `command_launch`, remove the `bash.py` hardcode, so io_boundary is consistently catalog-derived + consumer-time (never persisted). Cleanest but more work.\n3. **Persist the full catalog classification in the survey** — reverses the WI-fakuv/WI-puvun reframe (they deliberately made it on-demand). Largest; likely not wanted.\n\n## Already done (2026-07-15, commit 431bc21919)\n\nThe `io_boundary` MetaKeySpec axis description (`axis_meta_keys.py`) was corrected to state the persisted-vs-on-demand split, so the field is no longer silently misleading. This item tracks the remaining producer-hint-leak cleanup (disposition 1 or 2).\n\n## Related\n\n- WI-fakuv / WI-puvun (the io-boundary reframe, both done).\n- INV-lifik (parent investigation; re-verified after this split)."  # 196e
    fields: {}  # 196e
- op: discuss  # f76f
  at: '2026-07-29T01:38:46Z'  # f76f
  by: agent  # f76f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f76f
  clock: 2  # f76f
  nonce: f76f  # f76f
  message: "SCOPING ANALYSIS (2026-07-28, no code yet) — the filed three-way fork resolves to a fourth option that dissolves the tension. Banking it so the next session starts from the conclusion, not the fork.\n\nTHE APPARENT TENSION. The item frames the bash.py producer stamp as a 'leak' contradicting the WI-fakuv/WI-puvun reframe (io_boundary is consumer-time-DERIVED, 0 producer stamps). But the decision register (2026-07-05, WI-javoh) records that same stamp as an INTENTIONAL, explicitly sanctioned exception: 'a DOCUMENTED exception to the consumer-time-derivation contract ... sanctioned by ADR-0016 impl-note Ruling A'. So this reads as a filed item asking to reverse a standing ADR-sanctioned decision.\n\nWHY THE ITEM'S OWN OPTION 2 IS UNSOUND AS WRITTEN. Option 2 proposes 'add io_primitives/bash.yaml mapping shell commands -> command_launch'. That re-opens exactly the hand-curation the WI-javoh ruling CLOSED: 'the emission gate is the closed shell-builtin denylist ... Do NOT gate classification by an allowlist', and the adversarial audit there refuted a curated command list because 'there is NO clean invariant separating curl from git'. A bash.yaml enumerating commands is that allowlist by another name. Option 1 (strip at serialization) needs a separate producer-hint channel and is strictly more machinery. Option 3 reverses the reframe outright.\n\nOPTION 2' (recommended) — DERIVE FROM EDGE SHAPE, NOT FROM A CATALOG. bash.py already emits a fully distinctive edge (bash.py:520-538): edge_type='calls', is_resolved=False, dst='bash:<cmd>:0-0:<cmd>:unresolved', dst_ref=ExternalRef(lang='bash', module_path=cmd, name=cmd). compute_boundary_map already aggregates any edge carrying meta['io_boundary'] (io_boundary.py:1387-1393), whichever way it got there. So the rule 'an unresolved calls edge whose dst_ref.lang == bash IS a command_launch boundary' can be evaluated at consumer time with ZERO per-command enumeration — then the bash.py meta stamp drops and io_boundary becomes uniformly consumer-derived, with nothing persisted.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT A GOVERNANCE REVERSAL (the load-bearing point). Reading ADR-0016 impl-note Ruling A for what it DEFENDS rather than what it says: it rules that command-mediated languages populate the subprocess boundary BY EMITTING UNRESOLVED EXTERNAL-COMMAND EDGES, NOT via a data-I/O catalog. Option 2' preserves that exactly — bash still emits the unresolved external-command edges, and no catalog is introduced. Only the DERIVED LABEL moves from producer-stamp to consumer-derivation. The ruling is about how the boundary is POPULATED, not about where the label is stamped. So the A-vs-B framing is a FALSE BINARY in the WI-papag sense: both documents are right about different subsets, and the reconciliation is a refined mechanism rather than picking a side.\n\nSCOPE ESTIMATE: a shape predicate in io_boundary.py + drop the two bash.py meta keys (keeping io_primitive is a separate question — it names the command and has no catalog-derivation path, so it likely STAYS as an honest producer hint) + tests asserting (a) the persisted survey carries no meta.io_boundary at all, closing INV-lifik's zero-entropy finding at its root, and (b) 'hypergumbo io-boundaries' still reports the same command_launch_edges count as today (behaviour-preserving). Item stays todo_hard; nothing implemented."  # f76f
